<article class="env-lemma" id="01L6">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01L6">Lemma <span data-tag="01L6">26.23.7</span>.</a> Let $j : X \to Y$ be a morphism of schemes. If </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$j$ is injective on points, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
for any $x \in X$ the ring map $j^\sharp _ x : \mathcal{O}_{Y, j(x)} \to \mathcal{O}_{X, x}$ is surjective, 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then $j$ is a monomorphism. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $a, b : Z \to X$ be two morphisms of schemes such that $j \circ a = j \circ b$. Then (1) implies $a = b$ as underlying maps of topological spaces. For any $z \in Z$ we have $a^\sharp _ z \circ j^\sharp _{a(z)} = b^\sharp _ z \circ j^\sharp _{b(z)}$ as maps $\mathcal{O}_{Y, j(a(z))} \to \mathcal{O}_{Z, z}$. The surjectivity of the maps $j^\sharp _ x$ forces $a^\sharp _ z = b^\sharp _ z$, $\forall z \in Z$. This implies that $a^\sharp = b^\sharp $. Hence we conclude $a = b$ as morphisms of schemes as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>